WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:05.379
Riverdale is an undeniable hit, and there's
a lot about the show that really works when

00:00:05.379 --> 00:00:07.430
it's firing on all cylinders.

00:00:07.430 --> 00:00:10.860
As with anything, though, there are also plenty
of things that don't quite land.

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:15.049
To appreciate them both, it's time to talk
about the best - and the worst - things about

00:00:15.049 --> 00:00:16.000
the show.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.720
One of the reasons Archie Comics have endured
for so long is their reliance on broad character

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:21.740
archetypes.

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:25.570
If you take characters with clear-cut traits
and apply them in the right way, you can essentially

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:28.140
tell a story in any genre you want.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:32.180
The TV series has embraced this from the very
beginning, and the result is a show nimble

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:37.340
enough to pivot between horror, comedy, musical,
and crime drama, often within the space of

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:38.720
a single episode.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:43.280
The flexibility with which Riverdale can dip
into many different genres and themes means

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:46.690
it can also successfully juggle many different
plots at once.

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:51.070
When this is done right, you get an extremely
satisfying story with a long arc and just

00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:52.660
the right amount of tension.

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:56.800
Unfortunately, season two's Black Hood story
wasn't quite so well-juggled.

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:01.180
It seemed to wrap up midway through the season,
only to be clumsily rolled out again for an

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:02.920
even bigger reveal later on.

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:05.400
Sometimes Riverdale should let these things
go sooner.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:11.150
Riverdale ostensibly has four main characters,
or at least it did when it started: Archie,

00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:13.100
Betty, Jughead, and Veronica.

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:17.240
They're the two guys and two girls who ultimately
form the two couples at the heart of the show,

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:20.280
while everyone else around them serves a supporting
role.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:25.200
However, one supporting character has become
absolutely essential: the majestic and deliciously

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:28.500
venomous Cheryl Blossom, played by Madelaine
Petsch.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:30.010
"Mr. Andrews!

00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:31.520
Nice haircut!

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:33.160
Looking extremely DILFy today.

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:34.520
Is Archie home?”

00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:35.550
“Cheryl.

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:37.390
Yes, um…”

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:41.490
Cheryl enters the series as the spoiled rich
girl who gets to be the center of attention

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:45.270
because her brother died, and ultimately evolves
into so much more.

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:49.390
Petsch's gloriously indulgent performance
means Cheryl also gets to steal pretty much

00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:50.840
every scene she's in.

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:52.200
"Listen up, ragamuffins.

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:56.202
I will not allow Riverdale High's above-average
GPA to suffer because of classrooms that are

00:01:56.202 --> 00:01:58.760
overcrowded with underachievers."

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:03.760
This is a character who can introduce herself
without a hint of irony as "Riverdale's resident

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:07.760
It Girl" in one scene and then melt your heart
with vulnerability in the next.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:09.480
She's just that good.

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:14.400
One of the very first things we learn about
Riverdale's version of Archie Andrews - football

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:18.530
star and resident Good Boy of Riverdale High
School - is that he's been having an affair

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:20.170
with one of his teachers.

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:24.020
It's a sexy, unexpected element that adds
a little hint of danger to a character we

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:28.420
think we know, and it sets Archie off on a
promising path to being much more than the

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:30.300
clean-cut boy next door.

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:33.020
In the years since then, Archie's become even
darker.

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:39.171
"And so Archie, always trying to do the good,
noble thing, took another step further into

00:02:39.171 --> 00:02:40.171
darkness.

00:02:40.171 --> 00:02:44.770
And yet, despite all these steps into darkness,
he still remains the least interesting main

00:02:44.770 --> 00:02:47.560
character on a show in which he's supposed
to be the lead.

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:51.680
When the first footage of Riverdale arrived,
many viewers were quick to compare its visual

00:02:51.709 --> 00:02:55.810
tone to Twin Peaks, David Lynch and Mark Frost's
legendary TV show.

00:02:55.810 --> 00:03:00.250
Indeed, Riverdale's first major storyline
did seem pretty Twin Peaks-esque.

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:04.450
The show's drifted even farther into the horrific
in season two and it's made Riverdale even

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:05.760
more fun to watch.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:14.000
"It's killing season in Riverdale, and I wouldn't
be surprised if it was the same man."

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:18.680
The main Riverdale cast aside, there's another
group of key supporting players: Josie and

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:19.770
the Pussycats.

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:23.840
The trouble is, while Riverdale's version
of Josie often has a bigger role to play as

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:29.220
a supporting cast member, the Pussycats as
a unit don't get used very often.

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:32.780
Even in some of the show's more music-heavy
moments, they're pushed aside in favor of

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:35.550
the main cast, which is a real shame.

00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:37.610
Hopefully they get more to do in future episodes.

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:41.560
"This year, we'd like to build on that success.

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:46.280
Continue telling our story with songs we write.”

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:48.840
Riverdale is a show packed with high-stakes
stories.

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:50.310
A murder needs to be solved.

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:52.320
A vigilante is roaming the streets.

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:54.690
There's a gang war brewing on the South Side.

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:58.930
It works both because of the show's sincere
desire to tell these stories in all their

00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:03.769
juicy, soapy glory, and because of its commitment
to remaining tongue-in-cheek about just how

00:04:03.769 --> 00:04:05.590
soapy it can get.

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:10.330
That means that in addition to deeply emotional
and even terrifying moments, we get "dark

00:04:10.330 --> 00:04:14.500
Betty" in a black wig or Cheryl bringing the
cheerleading squad to a funeral in attire

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:17.689
that could best be described as "Spirit Mourning."

00:04:17.689 --> 00:04:21.739
Riverdale is never afraid to have fun with
itself, and that makes it more enjoyable all

00:04:21.739 --> 00:04:22.640
around.

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:26.880
When he was first introduced, Jughead Jones
was the kind of Greek Chorus of Riverdale,

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:30.460
a narrator filling in the viewer on the show's
various shenanigans.

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:35.760
"I'm the damaged, loner outsider from the
wrong side of the tracks."

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:40.659
Then, as the Jason Blossom story wound down,
Jughead became a guy who got mixed up in his

00:04:40.659 --> 00:04:45.039
father's biker gang and has been one of the
strangest characters on the show since then.

00:04:45.039 --> 00:04:47.009
In the character's own words:

00:04:47.009 --> 00:04:48.009
"I’m weird.

00:04:48.009 --> 00:04:49.199
I'm a weirdo.

00:04:49.199 --> 00:04:53.270
I don't fit in, and I don't want to fit in.

00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:56.020
Have you ever seen me without this stupid
hat on?

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:56.449
That's weird."

